        rules: {
          objective: 'Avoid taking tricks containing counter tiles; lowest total score wins. The name comes from the 11 points in play per deal (double-0 = 4, seven 3-suit tiles = 7).',
          gameplay: 'Leader plays a tile and nominates which suit (0-6 or doubles) is led. Others must follow suit if possible; otherwise discard. Highest tile in led suit wins the trick. Winner leads next trick.',
          keyRules: [
            'Pass 3 tiles left before play',
            'Leader nominates suit for each trick',
            'Follow suit when possible',
            'Lowest score wins',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Mark penalty tiles (3-suit and double-0) so they are easy to spot; the game feels different from Hearts—moon shots are more frequent due to smaller deck and every tile belonging to two suits. Follow suit when possible; avoid taking tricks with penalty tiles.',
          intermediate: 'Use double-0: leading it as blank guarantees winning the trick; leading it as double (if another double is out) can dump it. Lead low blanks to try to force 0-0 onto someone else, but beware of helping a moon-shot. The 3-3 is high in the penalty suit but can fall to a higher double.',
          advanced: 'When passing: passing high/doubles and penalty tiles to your left helps their moon-shot; holding doubles and penalty tiles can be the best defence. Defending against moon-shots is hard—penalty tiles can fall to other suits. Balance dumping points vs blocking the shooter.',
          tips: [
            'Paint 3-suit pips a different color (e.g. red) so penalty tiles are obvious.',
            'Leading 0-0 as blank wins the trick; as double you can lose it if another double exists.',
            'Passing high tiles left may give your neighbour a moon-shot; sometimes keep them.',
            'Lead low blanks to force 0-0 out, similar to leading low spades in Hearts to force the Queen.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
